Rach2: let any account run ping.

Only a privileged process may open a raw ICMP socket, here as on Linux, so
upstream's ping is root's. When socket(2) refuses one with EPERM, this patch
opens an ICMP datagram socket, Linux's ping socket, and the applet follows
what that socket does: the kernel writes the identifier and the checksum,
delivers only this socket's replies, and gives them without the IPv4 header,
so the reply's TTL comes from IP_RECVTTL and the ICMPv6 filter and checksum
offset are left unset.

ping6 asks for RFC 3542's IPV6_RECVHOPLIMIT in place of RFC 2292's option,
which the kernel does not keep, so that a reply's hop limit is printed. An
interval of 0, which stopped the interval timer and with it the requests and
the check of -w, sends each request when the reply to the last arrives, as -A
does, and after 10 ms when none does, as iputils' flood mode does. A shorter
timer can expire again before the handler that armed it returns, as one of a
microsecond does under KVM, where arming it takes longer, and the handler then
runs back to back and no reply is read.

Adaptive sends and retries run in the receive loop, which waits with ppoll.
Both paths check the send count before sending, so a late reply cannot
send a request past -c or restart the final wait. Each pass of the loop checks
-w and the final wait, sends at most one due retry and then polls, with no
wait when a retry is due, so that queued replies are read however short the
interval, and a busy socket cannot hold off a retry or the end. The interval
runs from the start of each send. The loop checks both ends again after each
read, so that a reply read after either is not counted.

